** AI — Units 294–301
Unit 294: “We used to think of camels as almost drought-proof animals,” said Dr Amina Yusuf, a veterinary researcher who has studied livestock health in arid areas of East Africa. “What we are now seeing is that even a species this well adapted has physiological limits, and those limits are being reached more often than before.”
Unit 295: Camel populations in parts of the Horn of Africa have continued to grow in absolute numbers, partly because herders have shifted away from more heat-vulnerable cattle. But researchers caution that rising ownership does not necessarily mean rising resilience: herd productivity — milk yield, calving rates and body condition — is, by several regional accounts, declining even where overall numbers remain stable.
Unit 296: Why heat is proving so damaging.
Unit 297: Camels are, by any measure, extraordinarily well suited to hot, dry environments.
Unit 298: They can tolerate body temperature fluctuations of several degrees over the day, avoiding excessive sweating. Their humps store fat rather than water, providing an energy reserve without the insulating effect of fat spread across the body. They can go without drinking for a week or more, then consume enormous quantities of water in a single sitting to rehydrate.
Unit 299: These adaptations, however, are calibrated to a particular range of conditions — conditions that scientists say are shifting.
Unit 300: “The issue is not simply that it is hot — deserts have always been hot,” explained Professor Bernard Kiptoo, an animal physiologist who studies heat tolerance in livestock. “The issue is the combination of higher baseline temperatures, longer heatwaves, and shorter recovery periods between droughts. A camel that could once rest and recover its body condition between dry spells no longer has that opportunity.”
Unit 301: Reduced, less predictable rainfall also affects the quality and availability of forage. Camels are browsers, feeding on shrubs and trees rather than grass, and prolonged drought reduces both the quantity and nutritional value of vegetation available. Weakened animals become more vulnerable to disease, less able to walk the long distances now required to reach water and pasture, and less able to regulate body temperature even within their normal range.

